[{:db/id 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-075cd559-2719-533a-90f7-057ee06afb2f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105620 :question/problem "Determine if ![](images/5d/5da5dddce497b4b2b8ffe24924714e960a3c43088351e0a2a6d039000ed78035.svg) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](images/6a/6a73978c951c4ae02d1455b5881a210c3d415bf1beef2f6f41f694c361708422.svg) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105628 :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector ![](images/8d/8dd3f3b1b65f44a9d1db82d12e4dbf25e764d85c8bdfcd4538750a0e51c41d5d.svg) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/78/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105622 :question/problem "An eigenvector of ![](images/3b/3b3b1d2eb2faaa02d5c758f820aa597ad2276365906ca16d701a42a0cdcda5af.svg) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](images/7c/7c5842e4b605873d4d965b3d5d8a504ba8bb8610ed8e78d9df0dd186232f9e3b.svg) Determine the value of $c.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0ddf6872-c9fc-59bb-a8a4-f3c287d11e0a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105621 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/62/625c1f7a98060333fba9bf4e4172787a6860b796e6de90a12ac2ae4e21611275.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/34/34580c90a404ad28b7fb44eb2eebd0465e6cbb2a8bbd339d1981a7dfc8f84a29.svg) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](images/20/2034a9bdc4a5f26258a8d72ff9496a72f98c9a9f755502318f2fd6ac0abc6553.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/60/60c921f21c6fc641a354cc91d398540c27f0e757bb59bc924f4f7ccaff11a731.svg) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-075cd559-2719-533a-90f7-057ee06afb2f" :question/id #uuid "075cd559-2719-533a-90f7-057ee06afb2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106776" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10=5\\lambda  \\\\ -8=2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7db53a96-c2b8-5320-933c-98ee29c6736b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7db53a96-c2b8-5320-933c-98ee29c6736b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7db53a96-c2b8-5320-933c-98ee29c6736b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-789dcf2a-206e-57f2-a9a3-9f05e8688d99" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15a7b8b4-79e0-5a5e-b815-4fb73d16b52a" "answer-b6451324-810e-5585-b20a-6b631b55ec25" "answer-eb0c0a14-6cf1-5cc9-93cf-929bd01a89bc" "answer-9f3222d4-dee2-5a55-8149-170c56576786" "answer-789dcf2a-206e-57f2-a9a3-9f05e8688d99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15a7b8b4-79e0-5a5e-b815-4fb73d16b52a" :answer/id #uuid "15a7b8b4-79e0-5a5e-b815-4fb73d16b52a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6451324-810e-5585-b20a-6b631b55ec25" :answer/id #uuid "b6451324-810e-5585-b20a-6b631b55ec25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb0c0a14-6cf1-5cc9-93cf-929bd01a89bc" :answer/id #uuid "eb0c0a14-6cf1-5cc9-93cf-929bd01a89bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f3222d4-dee2-5a55-8149-170c56576786" :answer/id #uuid "9f3222d4-dee2-5a55-8149-170c56576786" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-789dcf2a-206e-57f2-a9a3-9f05e8688d99" :answer/id #uuid "789dcf2a-206e-57f2-a9a3-9f05e8688d99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ddf6872-c9fc-59bb-a8a4-f3c287d11e0a" :question/id #uuid "0ddf6872-c9fc-59bb-a8a4-f3c287d11e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75724" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (but not $1$).\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4$ (but not $5$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(-3v)=-3(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2v)=-3(2v),$ and $2v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e96c0df0-7687-56d8-909c-da1e58046f59"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e96c0df0-7687-56d8-909c-da1e58046f59" :answer-field/id #uuid "e96c0df0-7687-56d8-909c-da1e58046f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2add26dd-e43b-52c7-b2dd-74816713310b" "answer-4d09cdfe-9545-5f99-9a96-7ea92e64cc13" "answer-273db6e4-b799-50bd-a66c-30f726dc163b" "answer-5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" "answer-a4868990-cd46-582c-81e2-72d1d4d50bee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2add26dd-e43b-52c7-b2dd-74816713310b" :answer/id #uuid "2add26dd-e43b-52c7-b2dd-74816713310b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d09cdfe-9545-5f99-9a96-7ea92e64cc13" :answer/id #uuid "4d09cdfe-9545-5f99-9a96-7ea92e64cc13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-273db6e4-b799-50bd-a66c-30f726dc163b" :answer/id #uuid "273db6e4-b799-50bd-a66c-30f726dc163b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" :answer/id #uuid "5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4868990-cd46-582c-81e2-72d1d4d50bee" :answer/id #uuid "a4868990-cd46-582c-81e2-72d1d4d50bee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107090 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n![](images/63/63e2c65a23a0680412a14084d543b6cc19c0529fd80863b09c99987d309eaa4f.svg) \nand the vector ![](images/b7/b7811b74d8d3075357925864b5be27f57b292dadca9c04d222954baa1e66ba2a.svg) Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector ![](images/13/13a28e543647ede7a88b30fb652bd7fba6c9adb8ee3142161a102de947ef2d2a.svg) is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106002 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
